**Read:** [Física III — Bernhard Johannes Lesche, UFJF](../sources/Fisica-III-Lesche-UFJF.lingua#r_0A3ZH3ZNQ5PGJ4Y05BSSCBAJE7), Hall effect and magnetic-force notes; [Classical Electromagnetism — Richard Fitzpatrick](../sources/Classical-Electromagnetism-Fitzpatrick.lingua#r_7NH7AZZH3NZ2FHJ4K5VBT49F6H), charged transport and magnetic response.

In a simple one-carrier conductor, transverse Lorentz force redistributes charge until an opposing electric force balances it. Derive the Hall voltage for a rectangular sample and state the direction convention, carrier charge, current direction, magnetic-field direction and thickness. Reversing current or B should reverse the ideal Hall signal; unwanted voltage offsets need not do so.

The elementary Hall coefficient 1/(nq) depends on the one-carrier model and its transport assumptions. Multiple carrier types, anisotropy and scattering can change the inference. A measured Hall sign or magnitude is evidence about a modelled transport response, not a universal direct census of microscopic electrons.

Use a four-terminal geometry, polarity reversals and uncertainty to separate longitudinal pickup from the transverse signal. Compare tensor Ohm law and reciprocity relations under field reversal. Connect this example to semiconductor/material characterization while keeping the full specialist studies distinct. Derive a prediction from geometry and declared parameters, then ask which alternative parameter sets could produce the same observed voltage before claiming a uniquely identified carrier density.

